In 1797, [[@thomas-jefferson|Thomas Jefferson]], who later became the [[@president-pm|third US president]], described some giant claws found in a cave in what is now West Virginia. He thought they came from an enormous lion and named it Megalonyx, "great claw". He hoped explorers might find one alive in the unexplored West.

It was soon shown to be a ground sloth. Scientists later named it Megalonyx jeffersonii in his honour, and it is now the state [[fossil|fossil]] of West Virginia. A few decades later, Charles Darwin dug up giant sloth bones in Argentina during his voyage on the Beagle.

