In early December 1952, cold, still air settled over [[@london|London]]. Millions of coal fires and factories kept pouring out smoke, and it couldn't escape. For about five days, a yellow-black smog hung over the city. Buses crawled behind people walking with torches. One opera performance had to stop because smog had crept inside and the audience couldn't see the stage.

Thousands of people died, mostly from breathing problems. Later studies suggested the true toll was much higher than first counted. In 1956, Britain passed the Clean Air Act, which limited coal smoke in cities.

{{story:lungs-breathe}}

In 2003, NASA's Chandra X-ray telescope found ripples in the hot gas around a giant black hole in the Perseus galaxy cluster, about 250 million [[lightyear|light-years]] away. The ripples are real pressure waves, sound waves, pushed out by the black hole.

The note is so low that humans could never hear it, about 57 [[octave|octaves]] below middle C on a piano. A single wave takes millions of years to pass. In 2022, NASA scientists raised the pitch by about 57 octaves so people could listen. It sounds like a deep, ghostly groan. An octave is a doubling of frequency, so 57 octaves is a doubling 57 times over, part of the [[@maths-music|maths of music]].

