Here the cat family splits. Lions, tigers, leopards and jaguars can roar. A lion's roar can be heard about 8 kilometres away. Their throats have a more flexible set of small bones and large, thick vocal folds that make deep, loud sounds.

Small cats, including house cats, have stiffer throat bones and smaller vocal folds. They cannot roar, but they can purr steadily, breathing in and out. Big cats can't purr in the same way. The [[@snow-leopard|snow leopard]] belongs with the big cats but can't roar at all, and the cheetah chirps like a bird.

