Phulkari was part of family life. Girls learned from mothers, aunts and grandmothers, often stitching together in the afternoons. A family might begin a bagh soon after a daughter was born, adding to it over the years so it was ready for her [[@weddings-india|wedding]].

Different pieces had different jobs. A *chope*, embroidered along the edges, was traditionally given by the maternal grandmother at a wedding. Other phulkaris were worn every day or kept for festivals. The cotton cloth, *khaddar*, was usually dyed deep red, rusty brown or indigo, and often spun and woven at home, just like the [[@khadi|khadi]] that Gandhi later made famous.

