In 1921, Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman was sailing home to India from Britain. Staring at the Mediterranean, he did not accept the usual explanation that the sea was blue simply because it reflected the [[@sky-blue|sky]]. He tested the water with a small optical instrument on board.

Back in Calcutta, he and his student K. S. Krishnan spent years shining light through liquids. On 28 February 1928, they announced that a tiny part of the light changed colour after hitting [[molecule|molecules]]. This became known as the Raman effect. It earned Raman the 1930 Nobel Prize in Physics, and 28 February is now India's National Science Day.

