Borglum first made a plaster model. His workers used a simple measuring system to copy it onto the mountain, so that every 2.5 cm on the model became 30 cm on the rock. Then they blasted. Nearly 400 people worked on the project over 14 years.

Workers sat in harness seats, hanging from steel cables over the cliff, drilling holes and packing them with [[@accident-dynamite|dynamite]]. After blasting, they drilled rows of small holes close together, then knocked away the rock between them and smoothed the surface. Despite the danger, no one died during the carving. Many workers were local miners who already knew how to handle explosives. Borglum's team hired a skilled powder man who could blast away rock to within a few centimetres of the final surface.

